      "text": "To find the range of the function \\( f(x) = \\arctan x + \\arctan \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x} \\), we start by simplifying the expression. Let's denote \\( y = \\arctan x \\) and \\( z = \\arctan \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x} \\). This means that \\( \\tan y = x \\) and \\( \\tan z = \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x} \\).\n\nWe need to find the relationship between \\( y \\) and \\( z \\). Recall the tangent addition formula:\n\\[ \\tan(a + b) = \\frac{\\tan a + \\tan b}{1 - \\tan a \\tan b}. \\]\n\nLet's set \\( a = y \\) and \\( b = z \\). Then,\n\\[ \\tan(y + z) = \\frac{\\tan y + \\tan z}{1 - \\tan y \\tan z}. \\]\n\nSubstituting \\( \\tan y = x \\) and \\( \\tan z = \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x} \\), we get:\n\\[ \\tan(y + z) = \\frac{x + \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x}}{1 - x \\cdot \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x}}. \\]\n\nSimplify the numerator and the denominator:\n\\[ \\tan(y + z) = \\frac{x + \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x}}{1 - \\frac{x(1 - x)}{1 + x}} = \\frac{x(1 + x) + (1 - x)}{1 + x - x(1 - x)} = \\frac{x + x^2 + 1 - x}{1 + x - x + x^2} = \\frac{1 + x^2}{1 + x^2} = 1. \\]\n\nThus, \\( \\tan(y + z) = 1 \\). Since \\( y \\) and \\( z \\) are in the interval \\((- \\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}) \\), the only value in this interval for which the tangent is 1 is \\( y + z = \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\). Therefore, \\( \\arctan x + \\arctan \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x} = \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\).\n\nHence, the range of the function \\( f(x) = \\arctan x + \\arctan \\frac{1 - x}{1 + x} \\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}\\).",
